{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "27241a16-fd92-4412-8e49-69dc477094e4", "team_id": "contest2026_286_hajiminanbeilvdou", "github_login": "Oliweitz", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 50, "ts": "2026-09-12T08:10:14.397Z", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "tokens_in": 80, "tokens_out": 463, "role": "tool", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "call_00_HFvM5IF5jvHs17BXDrzo6816", "input": {"command": "systeminfo 2>&1 | iconv -f GBK -t UTF-8 2>/dev/null | grep -i -A 6 \"hyper-v\"", "description": "Check virtualization support in firmware via systeminfo", "timeout": 120000}, "output": null}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "27241a16-fd92-4412-8e49-69dc477094e4", "team_id": "contest2026_286_hajiminanbeilvdou", "github_login": "Oliweitz", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 51, "ts": "2026-09-12T08:10:22.178Z", "role": "tool", "tool_name": "<result>", "tool_call_id": "call_00_HFvM5IF5jvHs17BXDrzo6816", "input": null, "output": "Hyper-V 要求:       虚拟机监视器模式扩展: 是\n                    固件中已启用虚拟化: 是\n                    二级地址转换: 是\n                    数据执行保护可用: 是", "is_error": false}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "27241a16-fd92-4412-8e49-69dc477094e4", "team_id": "contest2026_286_hajiminanbeilvdou", "github_login": "Oliweitz", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 52, "ts": "2026-09-12T08:10:34.478Z", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "tokens_in": 86, "tokens_out": 919, "role": "assistant", "thinking": "Virtualization is enabled in firmware, VM monitor mode extensions: yes, SLAT: yes, DEP: yes. All the hardware prerequisites are satisfied. So the issue is likely that:\n\n1. The Windows features (VirtualMachinePlatform / Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux) were just enabled by `wsl --install` and need a **reboot** to take effect.\n2. The Ubuntu distro didn't get registered.\n\nLet me check the feature states. Since Get-WindowsOptionalFeature needs admin, let me check the registry for feature states — actually, DISM feature state is in HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Component Based Servicing\\Packages or the CBS store. A simpler check: the `wsl --status` message said \"默认版本: 2\" and mentioned WSL2 kernel not usable — \"WSL2 内核文件未找到\" or \"当前计算机配置不支持\" — the text mentioned virtualization enablement.\n\nActually, re-reading the garbled text: \"WSL2 ... 未安装或当前计算机配置不支持\" + \"请确保在 BIOS 中已启用虚拟化\" + link to aka.ms/enablevirtualization. This is the standard message when WSL2 kernel isn't installed or the VirtualMachinePlatform feature is pending/disabled.\n\nThe cleanest approach: \n1. Check Windows features state via registry (non-admin): `reg query \"HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Windows\\OptionalFeatures\"` — hmm, that's not where CBS states live.\n\nLet me try a different approach: check whether the features are enabled-pending-reboot. \n\n`Get-WindowsOptionalFeature -Online -FeatureName VirtualMachinePlatform` needs admin. \n\nAlternative: `bcdedit /enum {current}` needs admin too.\n\nActually, let me just try to install the Ubuntu distro now and see what error comes back. `wsl --install -d Ubuntu --web-download` — this will also need elevation. Or `wsl --install --no-distribution` then `wsl --install -d Ubuntu`.\n\nLet me try without elevation first — modern WSL can install distros without admin for already-registered distros, but installing new distros typically needs admin when the WSL service isn't fully set up.\n\nLet me try: `wsl --install -d Ubuntu` non-elevated and capture output in UTF-8. If it errors with elevation requirement, I'll elevate.\n\nActually wait — first, does the machine need a reboot? When `wsl --install` enables the VirtualMachinePlatform feature on Windows 11, it usually says \"请求的操作成功。直到重新启动系统前更改将不会生效\" (The requested operation is successful. Changes will not be effective until the system is rebooted). The install completed with exit code 0. The wsl service might not be able to start VMs until reboot.\n\nLet me check if a reboot is pending: `reg query \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\WindowsUpdate\\Auto Update\\RebootRequired\"` — that's for WU. For CBS feature installs, check \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Component Based Servicing\\RebootPending\" and \"HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\" PendingFileRenameOperations.\n\nLet me check those registry keys (non-admin readable)."}
