{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "27241a16-fd92-4412-8e49-69dc477094e4", "team_id": "contest2026_286_hajiminanbeilvdou", "github_login": "Oliweitz", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 80, "ts": "2026-09-12T08:16:14.210Z", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "tokens_in": 213, "tokens_out": 908, "role": "assistant", "thinking": "Good progress:\n- The WSL Store package (MicrosoftCorporationII.WindowsSubsystemForLinux 2.7.14.0) IS installed with Status \"Ok\"\n- WSL client 2.7.14.0 with kernel 6.18.33.2-2 installed\n- The WSL core is there\n\nThe issue: LxssManager service missing → the `Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux` optional Windows feature is likely still pending reboot. With the Store-based WSL 2.x, the LxssManager service comes from the inbox optional feature.\n\nSo the machine really needs a reboot to activate the optional features. After reboot:\n1. LxssManager will exist\n2. Then `wsl --install -d Ubuntu` (or even just the distro registration) will work\n\nBefore asking the user to reboot, let me do one more thing — try to check whether the optional features are enabled. Non-admin trick: query the DISM package state via registry:\n\n`reg query \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Component Based Servicing\\Packages\" /f \"Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux\" /k` — that's a big query but /f filters. Packages with state 0x70 = installed, 0x60 = staged (pending reboot?). Actually the standard trick:\n\nState values in CBS Packages registry: \n- 0 = Absent\n- 0x10 = Resolved\n- 0x20 = Uninstall Pending\n- 0x30 = Staged\n- 0x40 = Superseded\n- 0x50 = Partially Installed\n- 0x60 = Installed  (hmm)\n- 0x70 = Installed Permanently\n\nFor features pending reboot, the feature typically shows as \"Enable Pending\" state. CBS feature state in registry: HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Component Based Servicing\\PackageIndex... complex.\n\nSimpler: `dism /online /get-featureinfo` needs admin. I can do one more elevated query via UAC... but the user is already getting UAC prompts. Let me just do a quick elevated check of both features in one shot — it's informative for deciding whether a reboot is strictly needed.\n\nActually — wait. There's a MUCH simpler non-admin check! The `wsl --status` output earlier said \"默认版本: 2\" (default version 2). But it also said things about WSL1 not supported on this config... Actually with the Store WSL 2.x, WSL1 support requires the inbox feature enabled.\n\nThe distro registration failing without error output is suspicious. Let me try running the distro install WITHOUT elevation and see the actual error message directly (it prints to console):\n\n`wsl --install -d Ubuntu --no-launch` — without admin, if the wslservice is available, distro install via Store package might work non-elevated (WSL 2.x allows per-user distro installs!). Actually yes — modern WSL allows installing distros without admin if the WSL core is set up. Let me try it directly and see the real output/error.\n\nLet me also set the output encoding properly. In Git Bash, `wsl.exe` outputs UTF-16LE. I'll pipe through iconv. But wait — if wsl prompts interactively it may hang; add --no-launch and it shouldn't prompt. Let me try with a timeout."}
